1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,095
You type a website name and a photo appears.
Let's slow

2
00:00:05,095 --> 00:00:10,190
that tiny moment down. Your computer does not
already have the

3
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:15,286
photo. Another computer stores it and can send
it back. A

4
00:00:15,286 --> 00:00:20,381
computer doing that job is called a server.
The line between

5
00:00:20,381 --> 00:00:25,476
them represents a way to exchange messages.
But there are countless

6
00:00:25,476 --> 00:00:30,571
possible destinations. Before asking for the
photo, your computer needs to

7
00:00:30,571 --> 00:00:35,667
know which address to contact. How does it
find that?

8
00:00:35,667 --> 00:00:40,633
Think about calling a friend. You remember
their name; your phone

9
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:45,600
uses a number. Websites have a similar split.
We type a

10
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:50,567
name, while the network uses a number called
an IP address

11
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:55,533
to help deliver messages. Watch the name turn
into an address.

12
00:00:55,533 --> 00:01:00,500
DNS, short for Domain Name System, helps make
that lookup. Our

13
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:05,467
pretend website is photos.orbit.example. The
address is made up for teaching

14
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:10,433
too. You don't need to memorize either one.

15
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:15,462
Your computer could ask, where is this
website? But who should

16
00:01:15,462 --> 00:01:20,490
answer? Meet one helper, shown in green. Its
job is to

17
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:25,519
find the address and bring it back. The
technical name for

18
00:01:25,519 --> 00:01:30,548
this helper is a resolver. That's all the word
means here:

19
00:01:30,548 --> 00:01:35,576
it resolves our question. Start with just
these two characters, your

20
00:01:35,576 --> 00:01:40,605
computer and its helper. Assume the helper
hasn't saved an answer

21
00:01:40,605 --> 00:01:45,633
yet. We are going to follow its search
together.

22
00:01:45,633 --> 00:01:50,600
A single helper cannot keep a fresh list of
every website.

23
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:55,567
Instead, responsibility is split into smaller
parts. Look at the dots

24
00:01:55,567 --> 00:02:00,533
in photos.orbit.example. Read from the right:
example, then orbit.example, then the

25
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:05,500
full name. It's like opening a large folder,
then a smaller

26
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:10,467
folder inside it. This nested arrangement is
called a hierarchy. The

27
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:15,433
dots tell the helper how to narrow its search.
No address

28
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:20,400
yet; first we are learning where to ask.

29
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:25,557
The helper begins at a starting directory
called the root. Imagine

30
00:02:25,557 --> 00:02:30,714
a reception desk that says, for names ending
in example, ask

31
00:02:30,714 --> 00:02:35,871
over there. It gives a useful direction, not
the website's final

32
00:02:35,871 --> 00:02:41,029
address. That direction is called a referral.
Follow the question going

33
00:02:41,029 --> 00:02:46,186
out and the clue coming back. The helper now
knows its

34
00:02:46,186 --> 00:02:51,343
next stop. In this drawing, each circle is a
job performed

35
00:02:51,343 --> 00:02:56,500
by DNS servers, not one unique machine for the
whole world.

36
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:01,529
The next directory handles the last part of
our name: example.

37
00:03:01,529 --> 00:03:06,557
That part is called a top-level domain, or
TLD. You have

38
00:03:06,557 --> 00:03:11,586
seen real ones such as com and org. Our
teaching name

39
00:03:11,586 --> 00:03:16,614
uses example. This directory points toward the
people responsible for orbit.example.

40
00:03:16,614 --> 00:03:21,643
Notice the pattern: ask a broad directory,
receive a narrower direction,

41
00:03:21,643 --> 00:03:26,671
ask again. The helper is doing this work for
us. Your

42
00:03:26,671 --> 00:03:31,700
browser doesn't need you to visit these
directories yourself.

43
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:36,729
Now we reach the keeper of the website's DNS
information. This

44
00:03:36,729 --> 00:03:41,757
is called an authoritative server because it
holds the relevant official

45
00:03:41,757 --> 00:03:46,786
record. In our example, that record pairs the
photo website's name

46
00:03:46,786 --> 00:03:51,814
with 192.0.2.10. Finally, a number! The
earlier stops were useful even

47
00:03:51,814 --> 00:03:56,843
though they didn't know it. They guided our
helper here. Watch

48
00:03:56,843 --> 00:04:01,871
the green answer travel back. We found the
address by following

49
00:04:01,871 --> 00:04:06,900
responsibility from a broad directory to a
specific record.

50
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:11,995
The helper gives that address to your
computer. Now your computer

51
00:04:11,995 --> 00:04:17,090
can try to contact the photo server. The name
lookup has

52
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:22,186
finished; asking for the actual photo is a
separate step. Think

53
00:04:22,186 --> 00:04:27,281
of finding a friend's phone number: you still
have to make

54
00:04:27,281 --> 00:04:32,376
the call. DNS hasn't delivered the picture
itself, and an address

55
00:04:32,376 --> 00:04:37,471
doesn't promise the server will answer. It has
solved one smaller

56
00:04:37,471 --> 00:04:42,567
problem: turning the name you recognize into
useful contact information.

57
00:04:42,567 --> 00:04:47,595
How long did that search take? A millisecond
is one thousandth

58
00:04:47,595 --> 00:04:52,624
of a second. Let's give our example four small
waits: two

59
00:04:52,624 --> 00:04:57,652
milliseconds for the helper exchange, then
twenty, thirty, and forty for

60
00:04:57,652 --> 00:05:02,681
its three directory exchanges. Watch each
colored piece extend the time

61
00:05:02,681 --> 00:05:07,710
bar. Add them: two plus twenty plus thirty
plus forty makes

62
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:12,738
ninety-two milliseconds. These are chosen
numbers, not promised Internet timings. We

63
00:05:12,738 --> 00:05:17,767
add because each step needs the previous
step's answer.

64
00:05:17,767 --> 00:05:22,862
What happens when someone asks the same
question again? The helper

65
00:05:22,862 --> 00:05:27,957
can keep a temporary copy of the answer. That
saved copy

66
00:05:27,957 --> 00:05:33,052
is called a cache. Now the long trip
disappears: in our

67
00:05:33,052 --> 00:05:38,148
example the reply needs only two milliseconds.
Ninety-two divided by two

68
00:05:38,148 --> 00:05:43,243
is forty-six, so this lookup is forty-six
times quicker. Only the

69
00:05:43,243 --> 00:05:48,338
lookup! We haven't made the photo download
forty-six times faster. Saving

70
00:05:48,338 --> 00:05:53,433
a useful answer simply avoids repeating work
we already did.

71
00:05:53,433 --> 00:05:58,529
But websites can move. If our helper remembers
an address forever,

72
00:05:58,529 --> 00:06:03,624
it might keep sending people to an old place.
So the

73
00:06:03,624 --> 00:06:08,719
saved answer comes with a time limit. Here it
is sixty

74
00:06:08,719 --> 00:06:13,814
seconds. Its name is time to live, or TTL.
Watch the

75
00:06:13,814 --> 00:06:18,910
timer count down. Reading the saved answer
does not restart that

76
00:06:18,910 --> 00:06:24,005
timer. Think of a note with an expiry time
printed on

77
00:06:24,005 --> 00:06:29,100
it: using the note doesn't make the
information new again.

78
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:34,195
Let's move the website while the timer still
has thirty seconds

79
00:06:34,195 --> 00:06:39,290
left. The official record changes to a new
address, 203.0.113.20. Does

80
00:06:39,290 --> 00:06:44,386
our helper magically know? No. It still has
the old saved

81
00:06:44,386 --> 00:06:49,481
copy and may reply with that address until its
allowed time

82
00:06:49,481 --> 00:06:54,576
runs out. The two boxes now show different
numbers. This explains

83
00:06:54,576 --> 00:06:59,671
why changing a website's DNS record can take
time to be

84
00:06:59,671 --> 00:07:04,767
noticed everywhere. Copies don't all update at
the same instant.

85
00:07:04,767 --> 00:07:09,333
The countdown reaches zero. For this example,
the next request makes

86
00:07:09,333 --> 00:07:13,900
the helper look again. This time it finds the
new address

87
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:18,467
and replaces its old copy. We show the
directory trip again

88
00:07:18,467 --> 00:07:23,033
so the refresh is easy to follow; real helpers
may remember

89
00:07:23,033 --> 00:07:27,600
some directions and skip steps. Our useful
rule is simple: reuse

90
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,167
an answer for a while, then check again. A
temporary memory

91
00:07:32,167 --> 00:07:36,733
gives us speed without pretending that
yesterday's information must stay true

92
00:07:36,733 --> 00:07:41,300
forever.

93
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:45,867
Now we can try a little mystery. Leave the
network connection

94
00:07:45,867 --> 00:07:50,433
working, but stop our helper from finding the
address. An app

95
00:07:50,433 --> 00:07:55,000
may fail before it knows where to send its
request. Meanwhile,

96
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,567
another task that already knows its
destination can keep going. So

97
00:07:59,567 --> 00:08:04,133
online does not always mean every website name
can be found.

98
00:08:04,133 --> 00:08:08,700
We have broken only the naming step in this
experiment. Real

99
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:13,267
failures can have other causes, but this
picture explains one possible

100
00:08:13,267 --> 00:08:17,833
reason.

101
00:08:17,833 --> 00:08:22,990
Let's shrink the whole journey into three
pictures. First, split the

102
00:08:22,990 --> 00:08:28,148
directory into smaller branches. Second,
follow directions until the right record

103
00:08:28,148 --> 00:08:33,305
answers. Third, remember that answer briefly.
The formal words are hierarchy,

104
00:08:33,305 --> 00:08:38,462
referrals, and caching. You already know what
those words describe because

105
00:08:38,462 --> 00:08:43,619
you've watched them happen. The countdown adds
one more detail: remembered

106
00:08:43,619 --> 00:08:48,776
answers need a time limit. These jobs work
together so no

107
00:08:48,776 --> 00:08:53,933
single directory has to hold every fresh
answer to every question.

108
00:08:53,933 --> 00:08:59,029
Return to the photo. A name you can remember
becomes an

109
00:08:59,029 --> 00:09:04,124
address the network can use. A helper finds
it, directories guide

110
00:09:04,124 --> 00:09:09,219
the helper, and temporary memory saves repeat
trips. That's the core

111
00:09:09,219 --> 00:09:14,314
of DNS. Next time you type a website name,
imagine that

112
00:09:14,314 --> 00:09:19,410
small search taking place before the page
begins to arrive. It

113
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:24,505
may finish almost instantly because somebody
remembered the answer. The website

114
00:09:24,505 --> 00:09:29,600
name looked like a destination; first, it was
a question.
